C++ templates using recursion and specialization to achieve compile-time computation:

```c++
template <int N>
struct Factorial {
  static constexpr int value = N * Factorial<N - 1>::value;
};

template <> 
struct Factorial<0> {
  static constexpr int value = 1;
};

template <typename T, size_t N>
constexpr size_t arraySize(T (&)[N]) {
 return N;
}
```

**Implementation Notes:** The compiler will recursively instantiate `Factorial` for each value of `N` down to the base case (`N=0`).  This generates distinct compiled code for each instantiation, eliminating the need for runtime calculation.  The `constexpr` keyword ensures the calculation happens at compile time. The `arraySize` example shows compile-time array size determination.
